#### Verified history and limits

The verified additions handoff records April 26, 2000, No. 98-1480, 529 U.S. 494: affirmance, 7–2. Thomas wrote for Rehnquist, O'Connor, Scalia, Kennedy, Ginsburg and Breyer; Stevens dissented with Souter. No partial joins or additional writing. The complete [Court opinion](https://www.law.cornell.edu/supct/html/98-1480.ZO.html), [dissent](https://www.law.cornell.edu/supct/html/98-1480.ZD.html) and footnotes were verified in that source review. Historical authorship and Rehnquist's vote are not imported.

The six affirming Associates match the historical civil injury rule: a conspiracy plaintiff must suffer injury from racketeering or another act independently wrongful under RICO, not simply any act advancing the agreement. This does not require each conspirator personally to perform the injury-producing act. Stevens and Souter match the opposing view that direct business/property injury from an act implementing a proved RICO agreement can suffice even if that act is not independently a RICO wrong. Agreement, injury and causation remain distinct under both views.

The historical footnotes preserve two important open questions. First, the decision does not resolve the investment-injury rule under subsection (a). Second, it does not decide whether a civil conspiracy plaintiff must establish an actionable completed violation of subsections (a)–(c), or whether a qualifying agreement plus at least one injury-producing racketeering act suffices. The frozen model's refusal to decide the exact coverage of other substantive wrongs must carry those reservations expressly into assembly. Neither possible rule is required to reject the presented termination injury, assumed for this question to be nonracketeering and not otherwise independently wrongful under RICO. The phrase independently wrongful may not be expanded silently into a requirement of every element of a completed substantive claim.

#### Entering-law reconciliation

The actual Holmes rule supplies factual causation and contextual directness, with no special racketeering injury, antitrust injury or automatic third-party bar. Steamfitters applies that rule to intervening smoking, illness and payment; it does not make Beck's asserted direct retaliation too remote by analogy alone. The majority construction therefore rests on a distinct civil-conspiracy injury requirement, as the model says. Scalia's earlier Holmes judgment-only position is not misreported as a complete opinion join; his later complete Steamfitters join supplies relevant support for the actual current rule.

Salinas establishes knowing agreement without two personally promised predicates and preserves Reves's substantive object. Its general criminal overt-act issue is expressly reserved in this timeline. The historical Beck discussion cannot turn historical Salinas's broader description into current law. All eight commitments retain that reservation while using the agreed distinction between criminal agreement and private compensable injury. This is a limitation on background precedent, not a reason to change any civil judgment vote. Rotella addresses accrual of a complete known-injury claim and supplies no new limitations disposition here.

